  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon features ridging beginning to \nbuild over the western CONUS/Intermountain West, with west-\nnorthwesterly flow pouring in across the eastern two thirds. Taking \na look at the surface, a cold front is currently place just south of \nthe NE/SD border region into northern Iowa while isolated to \nscattered showers linger across southeast Nebraska, where \nmodest surface pressure rises continue. A swath of shallow \ncumulus is in place over northeast Nebraska into Iowa, with \nlittle capping overhead. Wind/thermodynamic profiles in this \nenvironment show potential for wind/hail with a storm mode that \nwould grow upscale quickly. Though there is no inhibition, we \nare relying on modest subsidence and limited convergence along \nthe frontal boundary to keep the lid on any storm chances in \nthis area this afternoon/early evening. Otherwise, Temperatures \nhave recovered well despite initial cloudiness today, with highs\nexpected to push a bit further to into the upper 70s to low 80s\nacross the area, with increasing shower and storm potential \nthis evening and overnight hours continuing to the main concern \nof the short-term forecast. \n\nA surface low is currently located over northeast Kansas with a \nstrengthening boundary extending southeast, with that boundary \nforecast to shift into northeast Kansas over the course of the \nafternoon and evening hours. Additional lift across the High Plains \nis to lead to shower and storm development, shifting eastward later \nthis evening/overnight hours and decaying while it does so. \nConvective remnants from it alongside new storm initiation represent \nthe late evening/overnight shower and storm chances locally, with \nthe Kansas action holding the highest potential for severe activity \nthat could glance our area. As we push into the evening, the LLJ \nwill nose just to the north of the surface boundary, helping the \nstorms initiate and sustain themselves. Currently the best \nconvergence looks to be just south of the NE/KS border but is close \nenough to keep an eye on. The remaining showers wafting in from the \nnorthwest meanwhile favor central Nebraska as they  walk down the \nmain theta-e gradient. All in all, the morning convection has \nlimited our chances to only being along the periphery of our area of \nresponsibility, but it'll be close on the south and west sides.\n\nAdditional storms will attempt to develop during the morning hours \nin central Nebraska, spurred on by leftover lift from farther \ndisplaced High Plains convection, though timing has been shifting \nbased on how overnight showers transpire. Those chances will likely \nlinger into the early afternoon, though their ceiling will be \nlimited by decreasing instability due to the timing diurnally \nwith marginally-severe wind and hail being the primary hazards \nbefore diminish by the evening hours.\n\nThursday and Beyond:\n\nThursday takes a step towards the quieter side of the pattern with \nincreasing influence of the ridge to the west taking over. We'll see \nincreasing temperatures that peak Friday (mid-to-upper 80s), \nwavering a bit into early next week. Minor rain chances continue to \ndot the extended forecast, with no particular day being a home \nrun for rain chances locally.",
